WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.497
Missing data does not always appear in the same form.

00:00:03.497 --> 00:00:07.382
A null value means no SQL value is stored.

00:00:07.382 --> 00:00:09.527
A blank string is different.

00:00:09.527 --> 00:00:14.772
It is still a text value, but it contains no useful visible information.

00:00:14.772 --> 00:00:20.592
You may also encounter sentinel values such as ten zeros in a phone field.

00:00:20.592 --> 00:00:25.293
A system may use that value to mean unknown or not provided.

00:00:25.876 --> 00:00:29.283
Another example is the literal word Unknown.

00:00:29.283 --> 00:00:31.428
That is not a null value.

00:00:31.428 --> 00:00:35.978
It is an actual category value that communicates uncertainty.

00:00:35.978 --> 00:00:42.558
Why does this matter? Because we should not automatically treat all of these states as equivalent.

00:00:42.558 --> 00:00:46.503
Their meanings depend on the system and the business rules.

00:00:46.503 --> 00:00:53.538
Before standardizing missing data, identify how missingness is represented and document which representations are

00:00:53.538 --> 00:00:56.357
allowed to be converted to null.
